What the C actually does, and what I preserved:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mdriver[0m parses up to 100 ints with [38;5;10msscanf(in, "%d%zn", ...)[0m, advancing by the consumed-character count, and stops on the first failed conversion.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mcall_fma[0m computes [38;5;10mout[i] = ones[i]*data[i] + zeros[i][0m and returns [38;5;10mout[len-1][0m, so the printed value is just the last successfully parsed integer — or [38;5;10m0[0m when nothing parsed. That indirection is kept intact rather than collapsed.[0m[0m
- Output goes through C [38;5;10mprintf[0m via an [38;5;10mextern[0m declaration, not [38;5;10mprintln![0m. A Rust [38;5;10mstdout[0m buffer would not reliably flush when the library is called from a C host and would not interleave with the host's own output.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The one behavior worth calling out: glibc's [38;5;10m%d[0m converts the digit string with [38;5;10mstrtol[0m semantics, saturating at [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m, then truncates that [38;5;10mlong[0m into the [38;5;10mint*[0m. So [38;5;10m"9223372036854775808"[0m prints [38;5;10m-1[0m, not a clamped [38;5;10mINT_MAX[0m. I reproduced saturate-then-truncate rather than treating it as a bug.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
